bill good evening Congress does not have  much power to stop President Trump's new  tariff proposal so the focus right now  is on asking him to fine-tune it the  smarter way to go is to make it more  surgical and more targeted speaker  Ryan's comments reflect conservatives  heartburn on Capitol Hill over president  Trump's proposed tariffs on aluminum and  steel and their concerns of a possible  trade war we want to make sure that  abusers are held to account especially  China with when it comes to dumping and  trans shipping but we also want to make  sure that every step we take forward  does not create any unintended  consequences Republicans are eager to  run this year on passing tax reform and  they worried new tariffs could have a  negative impact on gains in the economy  there is a lot of concern among  Republican senators that this could sort  of metastasize into a larger trade war  Senate Democratic leader Chuck Schumer  says the action should be focused on  Beijing the president's instincts to go  after China are the right thing to do I  think China has taken terrible advantage  of America over the last decade or two  and they don't play fair Ohio Republican  Senator Rob Portman is a former US Trade  Representative it ought to be focused on  particular products in particular  countries and not be so broad-based we  ought to use more of a scalpel approach  and I think that would be better for our  economy today Treasury secretary Steve  minuchin doubled down on president  Trump's indication that there could be  fine-tuning for neighboring trade  partners to the extent that were  successful in renegotiating NAFTA those  those tariffs won't apply to Mexico and  Canada some outside conservative allies  warned in a letter to the president  today quote a new tax on steel and  aluminum will cost jobs increased cost  to consumers and forced businesses to go  overseas we strongly urge you to  reconsider this proposal some are  hopeful that pushback from outside  groups and the markets will help get the  attention of those at the White House if  the president does not listen to his  fellow Republicans here in Congress
 

